{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1779130850,"nonce":"f5E6HosDFIUok83PSNiyDGLF0oc=","message":"## Initial Direction: coordinated Scenario Workspace views\n\nAfter the Train/Evaluate collection UI review, the next product boundary appears to be structural/scenario authoring rather than more bottom-pane polish. The bottom pane should own pipeline execution, run/evaluation collections, status, logs, compute targets, and artifacts. The top pane should own the editable scenario: graph, task/workspace, mechanics, objectives/losses, metrics, probes, temporal selectors, and visual bindings.\n\n### Proposed Product Model\n\nIntroduce a coordinated top-pane Scenario Workspace with at least three synchronized views:\n\n- `Graph`: the existing node/port/wire model graph.\n- `Workspace`: a spatial/task/mechanics view. Use the name Mechanics rather than Biomechanics; not every physical system is biological. This view should show task objects such as goals, targets, cue regions, trajectories, obstacles, and physical/mechanics objects such as effectors, segments, joints, bodies, and muscles/actuators.\n- `Objectives`: a structured editor for losses, metrics, constraints, probes, rewards, and regularizers.\n\nThese views should project the same typed scenario/spec state. Selecting an end effector in Workspace, a state path/port in Graph, or a term in Objectives should refer to the same selector/objective record and cross-highlight the related objects in other views.\n\n### Why Not Node Drilldown as the Primary Model\n\nDouble-clicking Task or Mechanics nodes can be useful as a shortcut, but should not be the primary authoring model. Important interactions span multiple objects: for example, defining a loss between an end effector and a task goal, or a tracking objective between an effector state and a desired trajectory. If Task and Mechanics are isolated drilldown worlds, the user has to mentally stitch together the very relationship the UI should make visible.\n\nThe primary model should therefore be a workspace view that can show task and mechanics together, with graph-node drilldown as a way to focus or jump to the relevant object.\n\n### Scenario Cardinality Assumptions\n\nA reasonable first spec assumption:\n\n- A scenario has zero or one active task/workspace environment.\n- A scenario can have zero, one, or many mechanics objects.\n- A mechanics object can be bound to one or more graph nodes/components.\n- The task is more like the scenario environment than an ordinary computational subgraph: it provides unconditional inputs, targets, events, trial distributions, and success/reward/objective context.\n- Evaluation protocols are scenario/task variants, not static metadata cards.\n\nThis keeps room for multiple arms/plants, interacting physical systems, and future non-biological mechanics.\n\n### Objective/Loss Authoring Direction\n\nObjectives should be authorable from either Graph or Workspace:\n\n- Right-click or select a graph port/state path/edge: inspect, add objective, add metric, add probe, or navigate to related objective terms.\n- Select one or more workspace objects: inspect, add metric/probe, or define an objective between selected objects.\n- Select an end effector and a point goal: create a position/state objective with time/phase, components, norm, aggregation, and weight.\n- Select an end effector and a trajectory object: create a tracking objective with continuous/discrete tracking semantics, temporal alignment, metric/norm, and weight.\n- Select a thick/full-state graph edge: open a selector dialog first, then apply the same actions to the chosen state path.\n\nThe Objectives view should be the central place to review, edit, reorder/group, disable, or remove these terms. Visual marks in Graph and Workspace are projections of those objective specs, not separate decorations.\n\n### Top Pane Layout Direction\n\nThe top pane likely needs editor-group behavior rather than a single fixed canvas:\n\n- One-column mode: `Graph | Workspace | Objectives` tabs share the full top pane.\n- Two-column mode: one view on the left and one on the right, with a limited split. Example: Graph left, Objectives right; Workspace left, Objectives right.\n- Cross-highlighting and selection state should work regardless of layout.\n\nThis is similar in spirit to editor groups in development/design tools, but constrained to one or two columns to avoid turning Studio into a window manager.\n\n### Bottom Pane Contract\n\nMove structural editing out of the bottom pane over time:\n\n- Loss/objective definition belongs in top-pane Objective/Workspace/Graph authoring.\n- Task/evaluation protocol variants belong in task/workspace scenario authoring.\n- Optimizer, batch count, checkpoint cadence, compute target, run/eval launch, status, logs, and produced artifacts belong in the bottom pipeline pane.\n- Run/evaluation tables show outputs of specs; they should not be the place where sweeps/protocols magically appear.\n\n### Research Request Started\n\nA research sidecar has been started to look for precedents in CAD/CAE/simulation/game/editor tools and scientific/ML tools with synchronized graph/spatial/objective/property views. The goal is to import patterns for integrated multi-view selection, constraint/objective authoring, inspectors, editor groups, and relationship creation before drafting child issues.\n\n### Open Questions Before Child Specs\n\n- Should `Objectives` be a separate top-pane view from the start, or initially a right-side inspector/editor group that can later become its own view?\n- Should Workspace initially be schematic 2D, full canvas, or a simplified 3D/2D hybrid?\n- What is the first concrete task/mechanics pair to prototype: SimpleReaches + point mass, or two-link arm with end effector and goal?\n- How much task editing should the first prototype include: only target/go-cue/phase controls, or also target distributions and trajectories?\n- What is the minimal selector schema needed to let Graph, Workspace, and Objectives share references without hardcoding task-specific cases?\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}